WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.257 --> 00:00:01.760
Mastering a foreign language

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.930
is actually within everyone's reach.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:07.252
But if on the one hand mastering syntactical

00:00:07.252 --> 00:00:08.962
lexical and grammatical structures,

00:00:08.962 --> 00:00:12.621
handling verbs or tenses

00:00:12.621 --> 00:00:16.111
is something that everyone can achieve,

00:00:16.111 --> 00:00:19.105
on the other hand, just a handful of people

00:00:19.105 --> 00:00:21.097
can actually speak a foreign language

00:00:21.097 --> 00:00:24.091
with a native or native-like accent.

00:00:24.091 --> 00:00:24.870
Hi everyone,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.601
I am Luca, from LucaLampariello.com

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:29.670
and today I will talk to you how the fact that being able to speak

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.632
a foreign language with a native accent

00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:32.761
or native-like accent

00:00:32.761 --> 00:00:35.252
is not as difficult as you think,

00:00:35.252 --> 00:00:36.850
and I will talk to you how to pull it off.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.181
In another video I made on YouTube

00:00:41.466 --> 00:00:43.206
I explained how I succeeded in

00:00:43.206 --> 00:00:45.799
moving from having an atrocious accent in English

00:00:45.799 --> 00:00:47.736
to a native-like one

00:00:47.736 --> 00:00:49.306
in a relatively short period of time.

00:00:49.306 --> 00:00:51.477
And what happened is that

00:00:51.477 --> 00:00:52.789
in that crucial moment

00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:54.367
where I started moving from an atrocious accent

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:56.606
to a native-like one

00:00:56.606 --> 00:00:57.906
and in the moment when I started

00:00:57.906 --> 00:00:59.776
to speak almost like an American,

00:00:59.776 --> 00:01:02.107
my schoolmates as well as my friends

00:01:02.107 --> 00:01:04.057
used to make fun of me a bit.

00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:06.126
As a matter of fact, American English

00:01:06.126 --> 00:01:07.816
sounds more nasal than Italian.

00:01:07.816 --> 00:01:09.926
What happened is that

00:01:09.926 --> 00:01:11.616
my schoolmates found it strange

00:01:11.616 --> 00:01:13.745
to hear me speak that way.

00:01:13.745 --> 00:01:14.896
It was weird to them.

00:01:14.896 --> 00:01:16.456
And that was the moment

00:01:16.456 --> 00:01:19.277
that made the difference between speaking English like an Italian

00:01:19.277 --> 00:01:21.106
- as a normal Italian would -

00:01:21.106 --> 00:01:23.106
and speak almost like an American.

00:01:23.106 --> 00:01:25.396
Here is the thing: fundamentally I resisted

00:01:25.396 --> 00:01:28.456
to the social pressure of my schoolmates and friends

00:01:28.456 --> 00:01:29.986
who are members of

00:01:29.986 --> 00:01:31.406
"the Italian tribe"

00:01:31.406 --> 00:01:34.217
and of Italians, and I decided to

00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:37.135
acquire another identity other than mine.

00:01:37.135 --> 00:01:38.656
Renowned linguist Stephen Krashen

00:01:38.656 --> 00:01:41.047
- among other things -

00:01:41.047 --> 00:01:42.146
wrote an article

00:01:42.146 --> 00:01:44.826
on accent acquisition in a foreign language.

00:01:44.826 --> 00:01:45.967
In his article

00:01:45.967 --> 00:01:48.466
he makes an apparently disconcerting statement

00:01:48.466 --> 00:01:49.936
which, come to think of it, makes sense.

00:01:49.936 --> 00:01:51.778
Stephen claims -

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.565
and it is apparently a bold claim -

00:01:53.565 --> 00:01:54.587
according to which

00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:56.346
everyone - including adults -

00:01:56.346 --> 00:01:59.136
can acquire a foreign accent

00:01:59.136 --> 00:02:01.097
very quickly and accurately.

00:02:01.097 --> 00:02:05.477
They only choose not to,

00:02:05.477 --> 00:02:07.775
because they feel silly.

00:02:07.775 --> 00:02:09.127
And this has to do exactly with

00:02:09.127 --> 00:02:11.365
what I was saying a few seconds ago.

00:02:11.365 --> 00:02:14.915
Acquiring an accent is not just a merely linguistic matter,

00:02:14.915 --> 00:02:17.205
that is, it is not only about articulating new sounds,

00:02:17.205 --> 00:02:18.526
or hear them.

00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:22.016
It is, first and foremost, a psychological matter

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:25.006
and what has made a difference in my case

00:02:25.006 --> 00:02:28.656
is that I attributed the act of acquiring a foreign accent

00:02:28.656 --> 00:02:30.566
to that of acquiring another identity,

00:02:30.566 --> 00:02:33.086
and as a consequence of that, to my will of belong to the "American tribe",

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:35.466
other than mine.

00:02:38.146 --> 00:02:40.576
The will of belonging to another tribe

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.232
or embracing another tribe

00:02:42.232 --> 00:02:43.415
other than yours

00:02:43.415 --> 00:02:47.133
is a paramount psychological factor.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.332
But in all this, it is quite important

00:02:49.332 --> 00:02:50.775
to factor in the human factor. The people

00:02:50.775 --> 00:02:53.013
with whom we develop an accent.

00:02:53.013 --> 00:02:54.493
In my case, for example,

00:02:54.493 --> 00:02:57.342
acquiring a native-like accent

00:02:57.342 --> 00:02:59.321
coincided exactly with the moment

00:02:59.321 --> 00:03:01.634
when I started working with my tutor Susan,

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:03.062
of whom I always talk

00:03:03.062 --> 00:03:04.601
and whom I cannot thank enough.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:06.270
Spending time with her

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.792
helped me not only acquire her own way of speaking

00:03:08.792 --> 00:03:10.472
her accent and intonation,

00:03:10.472 --> 00:03:11.893
but first and foremost her way of being

00:03:11.893 --> 00:03:14.182
her way of behaving and relating to me,

00:03:14.182 --> 00:03:15.352
and to others.

00:03:15.352 --> 00:03:16.603
It is often said that

00:03:16.603 --> 00:03:19.233
we are the result of the 5 people

00:03:19.233 --> 00:03:21.716
with whom we spend our time.

00:03:21.716 --> 00:03:25.463
Now, I am not sure whether it is 5, 6 or 7,

00:03:25.463 --> 00:03:27.302
but I know that the important thing

00:03:27.302 --> 00:03:28.363
other than quantity

00:03:28.363 --> 00:03:30.631
is surely quality.

00:03:30.631 --> 00:03:32.523
It is then paramount

00:03:32.523 --> 00:03:33.851
when you acquiring a foreign accent

00:03:33.851 --> 00:03:37.312
and if you want to sound authentic in a given language

00:03:37.312 --> 00:03:39.922
that you spend time with people you treasure

00:03:39.922 --> 00:03:44.602
and that you build a certain identity around them.

00:03:47.802 --> 00:03:50.162
In his brief but intense academic paper,

00:03:50.215 --> 00:03:53.236
Stephen Krashen explains how language and accent

00:03:53.236 --> 00:03:55.034
play 2 fundamental roles.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.675
The first role is obviously

00:03:56.675 --> 00:03:59.322
that of allowing humans to communicate.

00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:00.615
The second role or function though

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:03.365
- and here Krashen talks more specifically about accent acquisition -

00:04:03.365 --> 00:04:06.636
is that accent is some sort of social marker.

00:04:06.636 --> 00:04:08.164
Come to think of it, the first thing

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:10.063
that an Italian notices and asks himself

00:04:10.063 --> 00:04:11.775
when he meets another Italian

00:04:11.775 --> 00:04:13.714
is "where does this person come from?"

00:04:13.714 --> 00:04:15.525
Does he come from Calabria and speaks like this? (accent from Calabria)

00:04:15.525 --> 00:04:17.105
Does he come from Veneto and he speaks like this? (Accent from Veneto)

00:04:17.105 --> 00:04:19.054
Does he come from Florence and speaks like this (Tuscan accent)

00:04:19.054 --> 00:04:21.013
If on the one hand, accent defines then

00:04:21.013 --> 00:04:23.247
the geographical region where you were born,

00:04:23.247 --> 00:04:25.615
on the other one, it also defines

00:04:25.615 --> 00:04:26.956
your social class.

00:04:26.956 --> 00:04:28.734
What I am trying to say is that

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.605
accent can be associated

00:04:31.605 --> 00:04:33.955
with a specific identity.

00:04:33.955 --> 00:04:36.684
If you want to acquire a native-like accent

00:04:36.684 --> 00:04:39.385
you have to identify with another human being (speaking that language).

00:04:39.385 --> 00:04:41.515
It is not just a matter of imitating that person

00:04:41.515 --> 00:04:45.215
but also BE that person, other than yourself.

00:04:45.215 --> 00:04:46.704
This is a paramount point,

00:04:46.704 --> 00:04:48.224
which also defines what you will do

00:04:48.224 --> 00:04:51.246
in order to acquire that specific accent.

00:04:51.246 --> 00:04:52.634
One thing I do often

00:04:52.634 --> 00:04:54.326
- and which I do less often these days -

00:04:54.326 --> 00:04:56.135
is to watch movies

00:04:56.135 --> 00:04:58.835
focus on specific snippets

00:04:58.835 --> 00:05:01.286
and imitate ad nauseam

00:05:01.286 --> 00:05:03.737
what and how actors utter sentences.

00:05:03.737 --> 00:05:07.561
It is not just a matter of imitating what they say

00:05:07.561 --> 00:05:11.327
but also to factor in all those things

00:05:11.327 --> 00:05:13.964
that accompany those words,

00:05:13.964 --> 00:05:18.245
such as their face, gestures, behaviour etc

00:05:18.245 --> 00:05:19.885
And this is quite important

00:05:19.885 --> 00:05:21.636
and gave me the possibility of developing

00:05:21.636 --> 00:05:24.125
a native-like accent

00:05:24.125 --> 00:05:26.025
in other languages,

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:27.828
other than American English.

00:05:30.425 --> 00:05:31.465
There you have it,

00:05:31.512 --> 00:05:33.141
I hope that you liked the video

00:05:33.141 --> 00:05:35.091
and that the information was useful.

00:05:35.091 --> 00:05:38.073
I wanted to do a quick recap.

00:05:38.073 --> 00:05:41.991
In order to acquire a native-like accent in a foreign language

00:05:42.227 --> 00:05:43.637
it is important

00:05:43.637 --> 00:05:46.758
1. To be ready and willing to embrace another cultural identity,

00:05:46.758 --> 00:05:49.397
without having to abandon one's own.

00:05:49.397 --> 00:05:53.087
2 It is important to spend time with people

00:05:53.087 --> 00:05:54.418
because it is not only sounds

00:05:54.418 --> 00:05:58.427
but experiences, smiles, glances,

00:05:58.427 --> 00:06:00.086
mutual comprehension, gestures

00:06:00.086 --> 00:06:04.540
that make and shape a linguistic identity.

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.986
And 3. Instead of imitating others like a parrot,

00:06:07.986 --> 00:06:10.008
take into account

00:06:10.008 --> 00:06:13.309
everything that has to do with human communication,

00:06:13.309 --> 00:06:15.177
so, once again, factor in things such as

00:06:15.177 --> 00:06:16.488
words and facial gestures,

00:06:16.488 --> 00:06:17.809
gestures and sounds,

00:06:17.809 --> 00:06:21.848
and try to imitate actors

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:23.428
imitate people

00:06:23.428 --> 00:06:25.667
and it should be like a game.

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:29.408
If you liked the video and want to know more,

00:06:29.408 --> 00:06:30.818
I have written an extensive article

00:06:30.818 --> 00:06:32.128
on this fascinating topic

00:06:32.128 --> 00:06:34.979
on my website www.lucalampariello.com.

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:36.937
How to land on the website?

00:06:36.937 --> 00:06:39.218
Well, you just need to click on the link

00:06:39.218 --> 00:06:40.498
in the description box,

00:06:40.498 --> 00:06:41.948
you can read the article

00:06:41.948 --> 00:06:44.577
as well as listen to the corresponding audio - all latest articles have audio and script

00:06:44.577 --> 00:06:46.668
and leave comments.

00:06:46.668 --> 00:06:47.837
So, I will be waiting for you there

00:06:47.837 --> 00:06:49.829
thanks again for listening

00:06:49.829 --> 00:06:53.306
and I'll see you very soon here on YouTube!

